      "text": "\n\n<think>\nThe user wants a technical guide for adding a new benchmark to an existing optimization repo. This is a practical, structured document. Let me think about what makes a good optimization benchmark repo structure and what the key concerns are:\n\n1. Directory structure - where scripts, data, notes, patches, results go\n2. Naming conventions for run directories\n3. Reproducibility requirements\n4. Validation criteria for promoting results\n\nLet me think about what a real optimization repo looks like. Think of repos like those for compiler optimization, algorithm optimization, numerical methods optimization. They typically have:\n- A top-level structure with benchmarks organized by problem\n- Each benchmark has its own directory\n- Scripts for running, data files, patches that implement the optimization, notes documenting the approach, and result summaries\n\nLet me structure this as a practical guide with clear sections. I'll make it concrete with examples rather than abstract. The tone should be direct, technical, no fluff. Like a CONTRIBUTING.md or internal wiki page.\n\nLet me think about the specific sections:\n\n1. Directory layout - where everything goes\n2. Naming conventions for run directories\n3. What to record for reproducibility\n4. Validation/promotion criteria\n\nI'll use a concrete example benchmark to ground the advice. Let me pick something like \"sparse matrix multiply\" or \"FFT\" or \"graph shortest path\" - something that feels real. I'll use a generic but concrete example.\n\nLet me write this as a clean, well-structured guide. Markdown formatting since it's a technical document.\n</think>\n\n# Adding a New Benchmark to the Optimization Repo\n\n## 1. Directory Layout\n\nEvery benchmark lives under `benchmarks/<name>/`. Create the full skeleton before writing any code:\n\n```\nbenchmarks/\n  <name>/\n    README.md            # one-paragraph problem statement + how to run\n    scripts/\n      run.sh             # single entry point; orchestrates everything\n      setup.sh           # fetches/builds data, installs deps\n      compare.py         # diffs two run dirs, prints a table\n    data/\n      raw/               # input files (git-lfs or external URL)\n      generated/         # synthetic inputs produced by a seeded script\n      README.md          # provenance: where each file came from, checksums\n    patches/\n      baseline.diff      # the \"before\" state (or a pointer to a commit)\n      <approach>.diff   ",
